  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure and upper level ridging maintains settled\nweather across the region. Tonight will likely have enough \nsurface flow to prevent much fog formation although some valley \nfog in the deep mountain valleys in the northeast mountains will\nhave a chance at fog development. Elsewhere will remain \nunaffected by fog development outside a few patches here and \nthere which is not out of the question. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAt the surface a cold front will advance into the region from \nthe northwest and move southeastward for Friday. This will \nprovide high chances for widespread shower activity and possibly\nthunderstorms as well. Isolated to scattered thunderstorms are \npossible and may develop in areas where low instability and low \nlevel convergence become maximized during the afternoon. The \ncombination of weak instability and low deep layer shear will \nlimit the severe threat potential. We cannot rule out a few \nstronger storms, however this would be very isolated in nature.\n\nThere will be enough DCAPE and PWAT values to support heavy \ndownpours for Friday, but we are not in an excessive rainfall \noutlook. However, isolated instances of possible flash flooding\ncannot be ruled out if a storms hit over a flood prone or low \nlying area repeatedly. WPC does have us in a marginal threat for\nexcessive rainfall outlooked for Saturday but this would be \nconfined to southern West Virignia and southwest Virignia. \nDepending how the frontal boundary meanders Saturday, which is \nmore south than previous forecast runs, will determine the \nseverity of any real hydro threats for Saturday.\n\nAs the front swings down to our south a low pressure wave is \nforecast to move along the boundary and promote more chances \nfor shower and possibly a few storms from late Saturday to \nSunday for the southernmost sectors of the CWA. However, as an \nupper level clipper type system moves into the region and pushes\na reinforcing cold front into our area the meandering frontal \nboundary will likely push out by late Sunday. This reinforcing \ncold front feature will provide stronger high pressure which \nwill filter out any activity across the area along with bringing\ndown temperatures values to below the climatological norm for \nthis time of year with 60s forecast for daytime highs across \nmuch of the area and 50s in the mountains.",
